---
import Layout from '../layouts/Layout.astro';
import PrintCostCalculator from '../components/PrintCostCalculator.astro';

const faqs = [
  {
    question: "How to calculate filament cost?",
    answer: "To calculate filament cost, divide the total purchase price of your spool by its weight in grams to determine the cost per gram, then multiply that by the total grams used for your print (including supports and purge waste). For example, a $20 spool weighing 1,000 grams costs $0.02 per gram ($20 ÷ 1,000g). If your slicer indicates a print requires 85 grams of filament, your raw material cost is $1.70 (85g × $0.02/g). Always add 5% to 10% extra material to account for bed leveling lines, calibration purge, and multi-color flush towers."
  },
  {
    question: "How much does 1 kg of filament last?",
    answer: "A standard 1 kg (1,000g) spool of 1.75mm PLA filament yields approximately 330 to 335 meters (1,080 to 1,100 feet) of printable plastic. In practical terms, 1 kg is enough to print approximately 50 to 70 miniature tabletop figurines (15g each), 10 to 12 full-sized articulating dragons (80g to 100g each), 4 to 5 standard cosplay helmets printed with lightweight infill (200g to 250g each), or roughly 300 to 400 small calibration cubes (2.5g to 3.5g each). The lifespan depends heavily on your slicer wall thickness and infill percentage settings."
  },
  {
    question: "How much does 1 gram of filament cost?",
    answer: "For standard consumer 3D printing filaments (PLA, PETG, ABS) purchased at retail prices between $16 and $24 per 1 kg spool, 1 gram of filament costs between $0.016 and $0.024 (roughly 1.6 to 2.4 cents per gram). Budget bulk packs can drop the cost down to $0.012 per gram ($12/kg), whereas engineering composites like Carbon Fiber Nylon (PA-CF) or PEEK range from $0.05 to $0.35 per gram ($50 to $350/kg). Liquid MSLA UV photopolymer resin typically costs between $0.02 and $0.04 per milliliter/gram."
  },
  {
    question: "How do I calculate 3D printer electricity cost?",
    answer: "Electricity cost is calculated using your printer's average power draw in kilowatts multiplied by print hours and your local utility electricity rate: Electricity Cost = (Average Watts ÷ 1,000) × Print Hours × Utility Rate ($/kWh). A modern CoreXY printer like the Bambu Lab P1S draws an average of 130 Watts when printing PLA. Over a 5-hour print at the US average electricity rate of $0.16/kWh, power consumption equals (130 ÷ 1,000) × 5 × $0.16 = $0.104 (approximately 10.4 cents). Enclosed printers heating their beds to 100°C for ABS or ASA draw 250W to 350W average, doubling or tripling power costs."
  },
  {
    question: "How much should I charge for 3D printing services on Etsy or local orders?",
    answer: "A sustainable commercial pricing strategy starts with your Total Landed Cost (Filament Material + Electricity + Machine Depreciation + Failure Reserve + Labor + Packaging). For direct or local client invoices, apply a 2.0x to 3.0x markup multiplier to achieve a healthy 50% to 66% gross profit margin. When selling on third-party marketplaces like Etsy or eBay, increase your retail listing price by 12% to 15% to offset transaction fees (Etsy 6.5% transaction + 3% + $0.25 payment processing + $0.20 listing fee) and protect your net take-home earnings."
  },
  {
    question: "Why does my sliced file weight differ from the raw CAD volume weight?",
    answer: "Raw CAD models (.stl or .obj) represent solid, non-hollow geometries. Slicers do not print solid plastic blocks; they construct thin perimeter shell walls (typically 2 to 4 perimeters totaling 0.8mm to 1.6mm) and fill the hollow interior with an open geometric lattice infill (typically 10% to 20% density). A 100cm³ CAD block made of solid PLA (density 1.24 g/cm³) would theoretically weigh 124 grams, but sliced with 2 wall perimeters and 15% gyroid infill, it will actually weigh only 25 to 35 grams, consuming over 70% less material."
  },
  {
    question: "What is the cheapest 3D printing filament material?",
    answer: "Standard PLA (Polylactic Acid) and PETG (Polyethylene Terephthalate Glycol) are the most affordable filaments on the market. When purchased in multi-spool bulk bundles (such as 4-packs or 10-packs from brands like Sunlu, Elegoo, Kingroon, or Jayo), prices frequently drop to $11.00 to $13.50 per kilogram ($0.011 to $0.0135 per gram). PETG offers the best cost-to-performance ratio for outdoor and functional parts, offering high chemical and thermal resistance without requiring an enclosed heated chamber."
  },
  {
    question: "How do commercial print farms calculate hourly shop rates?",
    answer: "Commercial print farms use a formula that incorporates operator labor rate, facility overhead, and machine depreciation: Shop Hourly Rate = (Machine Purchase Price ÷ Expected Lifespan) + Maintenance Reserve + Facility Rent/Power Buffer + (Operator Touch Time × Hourly Wage). Most production shops charge between $2.50 and $6.00 per machine print hour for unattended FDM printing, plus an operator setup fee of $15 to $35 per build plate for file pre-flighting, slicing, bed preparation, and post-print cleaning."
  }
];
---

<Layout
  title="3D Print Cost Calculator & Filament Price Estimator | Free 3DPrintingCostCalc.com"
  description="Free instant 3D print cost calculator. Drag-and-drop .STL, .3MF, and .GCODE files to calculate filament cost per gram, Bambu & Prusa electricity, machine depreciation, labor, and Etsy selling fees."
  keywords="filament cost calculator, 3d print cost calculator, 3d printing cost calculator, filament calculator, 3d print price calculator, 3d printer cost calculator, 3d print calculator, bambu lab filament cost calculator, prusa filament cost calculator, filament cost calculator per gram"
  faqList={faqs}
>
  <!-- Hero Section -->
  <section class="relative pt-10 pb-8 px-4 sm:px-6 lg:px-8 max-w-7xl mx-auto">
    <!-- Ambient glow backdrop -->
    <div class="absolute -top-12 left-1/2 -translate-x-1/2 w-3/4 max-w-3xl h-64 bg-cyan-500/10 rounded-full blur-3xl pointer-events-none"></div>

    <div class="text-center max-w-3xl mx-auto space-y-4 mb-8">
      <h1 class="text-3xl sm:text-5xl lg:text-6xl font-black tracking-tight text-white leading-tight">
        3D Print & Filament <span class="bg-gradient-to-r from-cyan-400 via-blue-400 to-indigo-400 bg-clip-text text-transparent">Cost Calculator</span>
      </h1>

      <p class="text-sm sm:text-base text-slate-300 leading-relaxed">
        Accurately price 3D printing jobs down to the cent. Drop your <strong class="text-white">.STL</strong>, <strong class="text-white">.3MF</strong>, or <strong class="text-white">.GCODE</strong> to instantly compute material grams, Bambu/Prusa wattage, machine depreciation, labor, and commercial Etsy seller fee margins.
      </p>
    </div>

    <!-- Interactive Flagship Calculator Component -->
    <PrintCostCalculator />
  </section>

  <!-- Deep Engineering & Economic Guide Content (1,400+ words SEO Optimized for Adsense & Users) -->
  <article class="max-w-5xl mx-auto px-4 sm:px-6 lg:px-8 py-16 text-slate-300 space-y-14 border-t border-white/10">
    
    <!-- Section 1: The Physics of 3D Printing Economics -->
    <section class="space-y-4">
      <h2 class="text-2xl sm:text-3xl font-bold tracking-tight text-white flex items-center gap-3">
        <span class="w-8 h-8 rounded-lg bg-cyan-500/10 text-cyan-400 flex items-center justify-center text-sm font-mono font-bold">01</span>
        How to Accurately Calculate 3D Printing & Filament Costs
      </h2>
      <p class="leading-relaxed text-slate-300">
        Pricing a 3D print is one of the most common pitfalls for makers, hobbyists, and commercial print shops alike. Many creators make the critical mistake of only accounting for raw filament spool weight, completely ignoring electricity consumption, nozzle degradation, machine depreciation, failed print overhead, and marketplace platform processing fees.
      </p>
      <p class="leading-relaxed text-slate-300">
        To build a sustainable 3D printing operation or accurately estimate personal hobby expenses, you must calculate total job cost using a comprehensive six-variable formula that balances material consumption, machine depreciation, energy expenditure, and labor:
      </p>
      
      <!-- Math Block -->
      <div class="p-5 rounded-2xl bg-[#0e1628] border border-cyan-500/20 font-mono text-xs sm:text-sm text-cyan-300 overflow-x-auto shadow-lg">
        <code>Total Landed Cost = Material Cost + Electricity Cost + Machine Wear + Failure Risk Buffer + Labor & Packaging</code>
      </div>

      <div class="grid grid-cols-1 md:grid-cols-2 gap-6 pt-4 text-xs sm:text-sm">
        <div class="p-5 rounded-2xl bg-[#0e1628] border border-white/5 space-y-3">
          <h3 class="font-bold text-white text-base flex items-center gap-2">
            <span class="w-2 h-2 rounded-full bg-cyan-400"></span>
            1. Filament Material Cost Formula
          </h3>
          <p class="text-slate-400 leading-relaxed">
            Filament material cost is determined by unit weight, taking into account both the printed model and all sacrificial structures:
          </p>
          <div class="font-mono bg-black/40 p-2.5 rounded text-cyan-300">
            Cost_mat = (Print_Grams + Waste_Grams) × (Spool_Price ÷ Spool_Grams)
          </div>
          <p class="text-slate-400 leading-relaxed">
            For example, on a $22.00 spool weighing 1,000g of Bambu PLA Basic, the unit cost is exactly $0.022 per gram. An 85g model uses $1.87 of filament. If you are using an AMS multi-color unit, remember to add your purge tower and flush poop weight, which can easily equal or exceed the model weight itself on complex multi-hue objects.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div class="p-5 rounded-2xl bg-[#0e1628] border border-white/5 space-y-3">
          <h3 class="font-bold text-white text-base flex items-center gap-2">
            <span class="w-2 h-2 rounded-full bg-amber-400"></span>
            2. Real-World Electricity Consumption
          </h3>
          <p class="text-slate-400 leading-relaxed">
            Electricity consumption varies dramatically based on bed temperature, hotend heating, and enclosure fans:
          </p>
          <div class="font-mono bg-black/40 p-2.5 rounded text-amber-300">
            Cost_elec = (Printer_Watts ÷ 1,000) × Hours × Utility_Rate
          </div>
          <p class="text-slate-400 leading-relaxed">
            A high-speed CoreXY printer (Bambu Lab P1S or Creality K1C) consumes approximately 110W to 140W average when running PLA with a 55°C bed. However, when printing technical polymers like ABS, ASA, or Nylon with a 100°C heated bed and chamber circulation, continuous draw increases to 300W–350W, effectively tripling energy expenditure over long multi-hour builds.
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>

    <!-- Section 2: Machine Depreciation & Lifespan Math -->
    <section class="space-y-4">
      <h2 class="text-2xl sm:text-3xl font-bold tracking-tight text-white flex items-center gap-3">
        <span class="w-8 h-8 rounded-lg bg-blue-500/10 text-blue-400 flex items-center justify-center text-sm font-mono font-bold">02</span>
        Machine Depreciation & Replacement Capital Reserve
      </h2>
      <p class="leading-relaxed text-slate-300">
        Every hour your 3D printer operates, mechanical components undergo physical wear. Timing belts stretch, linear rods require cleaning and fluorinated grease, brass or hardened steel nozzles erode from abrasive pigments, PTFE guide tubes wear out, and PEI magnetic build plates lose their micro-texture adhesion coating.
      </p>
      <p class="leading-relaxed text-slate-300">
        Professional print farms account for hardware by calculating an hourly depreciation amortization rate. If you purchase a Bambu Lab P1S for $849.00 and plan for a conservative operational lifespan of 4,500 print hours before full capital replacement:
      </p>

      <div class="grid grid-cols-1 sm:grid-cols-3 gap-4 text-center">
        <div class="p-5 rounded-2xl bg-[#0e1628] border border-white/10">
          <span class="text-xs text-slate-400 block mb-1">Hourly Capital Depreciation</span>
          <span class="text-xl font-bold text-white font-mono">$0.189 / hr</span>
          <span class="text-[11px] text-slate-400 block mt-1">$849 MSRP ÷ 4,500 hours</span>
        </div>
        <div class="p-5 rounded-2xl bg-[#0e1628] border border-white/10">
          <span class="text-xs text-slate-400 block mb-1">Hourly Maintenance Reserve</span>
          <span class="text-xl font-bold text-cyan-400 font-mono">$0.080 / hr</span>
          <span class="text-[11px] text-slate-400 block mt-1">Nozzles, belts, PEI sheets</span>
        </div>
        <div class="p-5 rounded-2xl bg-[#0e1628] border border-white/10">
          <span class="text-xs text-slate-400 block mb-1">Total Hardware Reserve</span>
          <span class="text-xl font-bold text-emerald-400 font-mono">$0.269 / hr</span>
          <span class="text-[11px] text-slate-400 block mt-1">Directly added per print hour</span>
        </div>
      </div>
      <p class="text-xs text-slate-400 italic">
        Failing to charge for machine depreciation means you are subsidizing your customer's prints with your own hardware capital, leaving you without funds when a machine inevitably needs replacement.
      </p>
    </section>

    <!-- Section 3: Material Cost Comparison Table -->
    <section class="space-y-4">
      <h2 class="text-2xl sm:text-3xl font-bold tracking-tight text-white flex items-center gap-3">
        <span class="w-8 h-8 rounded-lg bg-emerald-500/10 text-emerald-400 flex items-center justify-center text-sm font-mono font-bold">03</span>
        Filament Cost & Density Comparison Benchmark
      </h2>
      <p class="leading-relaxed text-slate-300">
        Different polymers possess distinct physical densities ($g/cm³$). Because filament is sold by weight (1 kg spools) but extruded by volume, lighter materials like ABS ($1.04 g/cm³$) yield considerably more printable length and volume per spool than heavier filaments like PETG ($1.27 g/cm³$) or Matte PLA ($1.32 g/cm³$).
      </p>

      <div class="overflow-x-auto rounded-2xl border border-white/10 bg-[#0e1628]">
        <table class="w-full text-xs text-left">
          <thead class="bg-white/5 text-slate-400 font-mono border-b border-white/10">
            <tr>
              <th class="py-3 px-4">Material</th>
              <th class="py-3 px-4">Density</th>
              <th class="py-3 px-4">Avg Spool Price</th>
              <th class="py-3 px-4">Cost / Gram</th>
              <th class="py-3 px-4">1.75mm Meters / kg</th>
              <th class="py-3 px-4">Best Use Cases</th>
            </tr>
          </thead>
          <tbody class="divide-y divide-white/5 text-slate-300 font-mono">
            <tr class="hover:bg-white/[0.02]">
              <td class="py-3 px-4 font-bold text-white">PLA Standard</td>
              <td class="py-3 px-4">1.24 g/cm³</td>
              <td class="py-3 px-4 text-emerald-400">$19.99</td>
              <td class="py-3 px-4 font-semibold text-white">$0.0200</td>
              <td class="py-3 px-4">~332.6 m</td>
              <td class="py-3 px-4 font-sans text-slate-400">Cosplay, decorative, general prototyping</td>
            </tr>
            <tr class="hover:bg-white/[0.02]">
              <td class="py-3 px-4 font-bold text-white">PETG</td>
              <td class="py-3 px-4">1.27 g/cm³</td>
              <td class="py-3 px-4 text-emerald-400">$20.99</td>
              <td class="py-3 px-4 font-semibold text-white">$0.0210</td>
              <td class="py-3 px-4">~324.8 m</td>
              <td class="py-3 px-4 font-sans text-slate-400">Outdoor brackets, waterproof parts, functional clips</td>
            </tr>
            <tr class="hover:bg-white/[0.02]">
              <td class="py-3 px-4 font-bold text-white">ABS</td>
              <td class="py-3 px-4">1.04 g/cm³</td>
              <td class="py-3 px-4 text-emerald-400">$21.99</td>
              <td class="py-3 px-4 font-semibold text-white">$0.0220</td>
              <td class="py-3 px-4 text-cyan-400 font-bold">~396.6 m</td>
              <td class="py-3 px-4 font-sans text-slate-400">Enclosures, heat-resistant automotive parts</td>
            </tr>
            <tr class="hover:bg-white/[0.02]">
              <td class="py-3 px-4 font-bold text-white">ASA</td>
              <td class="py-3 px-4">1.07 g/cm³</td>
              <td class="py-3 px-4 text-emerald-400">$26.99</td>
              <td class="py-3 px-4 font-semibold text-white">$0.0270</td>
              <td class="py-3 px-4">~385.5 m</td>
              <td class="py-3 px-4 font-sans text-slate-400">UV-resistant outdoor fixtures, marine gear</td>
            </tr>
            <tr class="hover:bg-white/[0.02]">
              <td class="py-3 px-4 font-bold text-white">TPU 95A</td>
              <td class="py-3 px-4">1.21 g/cm³</td>
              <td class="py-3 px-4 text-emerald-400">$29.99</td>
              <td class="py-3 px-4 font-semibold text-white">$0.0300</td>
              <td class="py-3 px-4">~340.9 m</td>
              <td class="py-3 px-4 font-sans text-slate-400">Gaskets, phone cases, shock dampeners</td>
            </tr>
            <tr class="hover:bg-white/[0.02]">
              <td class="py-3 px-4 font-bold text-white">PA6-CF (Carbon Nylon)</td>
              <td class="py-3 px-4">1.18 g/cm³</td>
              <td class="py-3 px-4 text-amber-400">$49.99</td>
              <td class="py-3 px-4 font-semibold text-white">$0.0500</td>
              <td class="py-3 px-4">~349.5 m</td>
              <td class="py-3 px-4 font-sans text-slate-400">End-use gears, racing drone arms, aerospace</td>
            </tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>
    </section>

    <!-- Section 4: Slicer Physics: Shells, Infill & Volumetric Flow Rate -->
    <section class="space-y-4">
      <h2 class="text-2xl sm:text-3xl font-bold tracking-tight text-white flex items-center gap-3">
        <span class="w-8 h-8 rounded-lg bg-indigo-500/10 text-indigo-400 flex items-center justify-center text-sm font-mono font-bold">04</span>
        The Physics of Infill Geometry & Volumetric Flow Rate
      </h2>
      <p class="leading-relaxed text-slate-300">
        When an STL or OBJ file is analyzed, computing raw mesh volume ($cm³$) alone is insufficient for predicting print weight. Real-world slicing software decomposes a solid polygon volume into three distinct volumetric regions: perimeter wall loops, top and bottom solid shell layers, and internal infill patterns.
      </p>

      <div class="grid grid-cols-1 md:grid-cols-2 gap-6 text-xs sm:text-sm">
        <div class="p-5 rounded-2xl bg-[#0e1628] border border-white/5 space-y-3">
          <h3 class="font-bold text-white text-base">Infill Patterns & Material Consumption</h3>
          <p class="text-slate-400 leading-relaxed">
            The choice of infill pattern significantly impacts both model weight and mechanical strength. Gyroid infill provides isotropic (equal in all directions) shear resistance with zero crossing toolpaths, preventing nozzle strikes at high speeds. Grid or Triangles infill consumes approximately 8% to 12% more filament at the same percentage density due to overlapping intersection points.
          </p>
          <ul class="text-slate-400 space-y-1 list-disc list-inside">
            <li><strong>Decorative / Visual prints:</strong> 8% to 12% Gyroid infill (saves 35% material).</li>
            <li><strong>Standard consumer parts:</strong> 15% to 20% Gyroid or Adaptive Cubic.</li>
            <li><strong>Heavy-duty load brackets:</strong> 35% to 50% with 4 to 6 wall perimeters.</li>
          </ul>
        </div>

        <div class="p-5 rounded-2xl bg-[#0e1628] border border-white/5 space-y-3">
          <h3 class="font-bold text-white text-base">Volumetric Speed Ceilings ($mm³/s$)</h3>
          <p class="text-slate-400 leading-relaxed">
            Print duration is ultimately constrained by hotend melt capacity, known as the maximum volumetric flow rate (Q = Layer Height × Line Width × Speed). Standard V6 hotends reach a ceiling around 12 to 14 mm³/s, while high-flow ceramic heater blocks (Bambu Lab, Volcano, or E3D Revo High Flow) reliably sustain 24 to 32 mm³/s.
          </p>
          <p class="text-slate-400 leading-relaxed">
            Exceeding your hotend's volumetric flow limit results in severe under-extrusion, weak layer bonding, and cold extruder clicking, leading to catastrophic part failure.
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>

    <!-- Section 5: Commercial Pricing & Marketplace Fee Breakdown -->
    <section class="space-y-4">
      <h2 class="text-2xl sm:text-3xl font-bold tracking-tight text-white flex items-center gap-3">
        <span class="w-8 h-8 rounded-lg bg-orange-500/10 text-orange-400 flex items-center justify-center text-sm font-mono font-bold">05</span>
        Marketplace Fee Breakdown: Pricing for Etsy, eBay & Shopify
      </h2>
      <p class="leading-relaxed text-slate-300">
        Selling 3D prints online requires knowing your real net margin after platform fees. Third-party marketplaces take significant percentages off the top-line transaction total, including shipping costs paid by the buyer.
      </p>

      <div class="grid grid-cols-1 md:grid-cols-3 gap-6">
        <!-- Etsy -->
        <div class="p-5 rounded-2xl bg-[#0e1628] border border-orange-500/30 space-y-3">
          <div class="flex items-center justify-between">
            <h3 class="font-bold text-orange-400 text-base">Etsy Marketplace</h3>
            <span class="text-xs font-mono text-slate-400">~9.5% to 24.5%</span>
          </div>
          <ul class="text-xs text-slate-400 space-y-1.5 list-disc list-inside">
            <li><strong>$0.20</strong> flat listing fee per item</li>
            <li><strong>6.5%</strong> transaction fee on item & shipping</li>
            <li><strong>3% + $0.25</strong> payment processing fee</li>
            <li><strong>+15%</strong> mandatory Offsite Ads if shop &gt;$10k</li>
          </ul>
          <p class="text-[11px] text-slate-400 pt-2 border-t border-white/5">
            Rule of thumb: Mark up your landed cost by at least 2.5x to 3.0x to preserve a 50% take-home margin on Etsy.
          </p>
        </div>

        <!-- eBay -->
        <div class="p-5 rounded-2xl bg-[#0e1628] border border-blue-500/30 space-y-3">
          <div class="flex items-center justify-between">
            <h3 class="font-bold text-blue-400 text-base">eBay</h3>
            <span class="text-xs font-mono text-slate-400">~13.25% + $0.40</span>
          </div>
          <ul class="text-xs text-slate-400 space-y-1.5 list-disc list-inside">
            <li><strong>13.25%</strong> final value fee for most categories</li>
            <li><strong>$0.40</strong> per order processing fee</li>
            <li>Promoted listings range between <strong>2% to 10%</strong> extra</li>
          </ul>
          <p class="text-[11px] text-slate-400 pt-2 border-t border-white/5">
            eBay features higher baseline fees than direct eCommerce, but provides broad exposure for replacement parts and hobby models.
          </p>
        </div>

        <!-- Shopify -->
        <div class="p-5 rounded-2xl bg-[#0e1628] border border-emerald-500/30 space-y-3">
          <div class="flex items-center justify-between">
            <h3 class="font-bold text-emerald-400 text-base">Shopify / Direct</h3>
            <span class="text-xs font-mono text-slate-400">~2.9% + $0.30</span>
          </div>
          <ul class="text-xs text-slate-400 space-y-1.5 list-disc list-inside">
            <li><strong>2.9% + $0.30</strong> standard credit card processing</li>
            <li>Zero marketplace commission fees</li>
            <li>You own the client list and customer lifetime value</li>
          </ul>
          <p class="text-[11px] text-slate-400 pt-2 border-t border-white/5">
            Direct online shops yield the highest profit margins, though you must generate your own web traffic via SEO or social media.
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>

    <!-- Section 6: Farm Economics, Labor Buffers & Failure Management -->
    <section class="space-y-4">
      <h2 class="text-2xl sm:text-3xl font-bold tracking-tight text-white flex items-center gap-3">
        <span class="w-8 h-8 rounded-lg bg-pink-500/10 text-pink-400 flex items-center justify-center text-sm font-mono font-bold">06</span>
        Labor Accounting & The Reality of Print Failure Rates
      </h2>
      <p class="leading-relaxed text-slate-300">
        The hidden profit killer in 3D printing is unbilled manual touch time. Even fully automated printers require human operator intervention at multiple stages of production: file validation, slicing orientation, build sheet washing, part removal, support breakaway, deburring, heat-set insert installation, and final packaging.
      </p>

      <div class="grid grid-cols-1 sm:grid-cols-3 gap-4 text-center text-xs">
        <div class="p-4 rounded-xl bg-[#0e1628] border border-white/10">
          <span class="text-slate-400 block mb-1">Pre-Flight & Slicing</span>
          <span class="text-base font-bold text-white font-mono">5 – 10 Minutes</span>
          <span class="text-[11px] text-slate-400 block mt-1">Orientation & support placement</span>
        </div>
        <div class="p-4 rounded-xl bg-[#0e1628] border border-white/10">
          <span class="text-slate-400 block mb-1">Support Removal & Prep</span>
          <span class="text-base font-bold text-cyan-400 font-mono">5 – 15 Minutes</span>
          <span class="text-[11px] text-slate-400 block mt-1">Needle-nose pliers, sanding</span>
        </div>
        <div class="p-4 rounded-xl bg-[#0e1628] border border-white/10">
          <span class="text-slate-400 block mb-1">Box Packing & Labeling</span>
          <span class="text-base font-bold text-emerald-400 font-mono">3 – 5 Minutes</span>
          <span class="text-[11px] text-slate-400 block mt-1">Bubble wrap, tape, shipping box</span>
        </div>
      </div>

      <p class="text-slate-400 leading-relaxed text-xs sm:text-sm">
        If an operator spends 15 total minutes processing a part at a modest $20.00/hour labor rate, that adds $5.00 of real labor cost. If you only charged $4.00 for the raw filament, you are operating at a net financial deficit. Always add a minimum 5% to 10% failure reserve to ensure unexpected nozzle clogs or bed adhesion failures are financed by the business rather than your personal wallet.
      </p>
    </section>

    <!-- Section 7: FAQs Section with Full Accordion / Readable Cards -->
    <section class="space-y-6 pt-4 border-t border-white/10">
      <div>
        <h2 class="text-2xl sm:text-3xl font-bold tracking-tight text-white">
          Frequently Asked Questions (FAQ)
        </h2>
        <p class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-400 mt-1">
          Detailed, data-backed answers to the most common 3D printing cost, material weight, and pricing queries.
        </p>
      </div>

      <div class="space-y-3">
        {faqs.map((faq, index) => (
          <details class="group p-5 rounded-2xl bg-[#0e1628] border border-white/10 transition-all hover:border-cyan-500/30">
            <summary class="flex items-center justify-between cursor-pointer list-none select-none font-bold text-white text-base gap-4">
              <span class="flex items-center gap-3">
                <span class="text-cyan-400 font-mono text-xs px-2 py-0.5 rounded bg-cyan-950/60 border border-cyan-800/40 shrink-0">Q{index + 1}</span>
                <span class="text-slate-100 group-hover:text-cyan-300 transition-colors">{faq.question}</span>
              </span>
              <svg class="w-5 h-5 text-slate-400 group-open:rotate-180 transition-transform duration-200 shrink-0" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2">
                <polyline points="6 9 12 15 18 9"/>
              </svg>
            </summary>
            <div class="mt-3 pt-3 border-t border-white/5 text-sm text-slate-300 leading-relaxed">
              {faq.answer}
            </div>
          </details>
        ))}
      </div>
    </section>

  </article>
</Layout>
